      "headline": "The Flash No One Could Explain: Vela’s South Atlantic Signal",
      "deck": "A U.S. satellite detected a double flash that resembled a nuclear-test signature, but an official panel could not determine what caused it.",
      "wikiImage": null,
      "fallbackSvg": null,
      "caption": "Vela 6911 recorded the disputed optical signal over a remote region of the South Atlantic.",
      "lore": "The Vela Incident became a Cold War case study in the difference between detecting an unusual signal and identifying its cause.",
      "body": "<p>At approximately 00:53 UTC on September 22, 1979, the U.S. Vela satellite 6911 registered a brief double flash over a remote region of the South Atlantic. Its optical sensors recorded several features resembling signals previously associated with atmospheric nuclear explosions.</p><p>Some U.S. intelligence officials initially interpreted the event as likely evidence of a clandestine nuclear test. A later CIA assessment reportedly assigned that interpretation a probability greater than 90 percent. Observations from the Arecibo Observatory were considered suggestive by some agencies, but they were not accepted by the official panel as clear corroboration.</p><p>A White House-sponsored panel chaired by Jack Ruina reported on January 7, 1980, that it could not determine whether the signal came from a nuclear explosion. The panel found that the signal had the general character of nuclear-explosion signals, but also contained a discrepancy that raised doubt about a nuclear origin.</p><blockquote>“the Panel cannot determine whether or not the light signal that was recorded by one VELA satellite ... was generated by a nuclear explosion.”</blockquote><p>The event demonstrated the reach of space-based nuclear-test surveillance and the limits of interpreting a single sensor record. It established that Vela 6911 detected a real and unusual optical signal, but did not conclusively establish that a nuclear explosion occurred, where it occurred, who or what caused it, or whether a natural explanation was responsible.</p>",
      "wrongBelief": "Some U.S. intelligence officials initially believed that the flash most likely represented a clandestine nuclear test.",
